### Abstract and Research Highlights

This study demonstrates the metabolic engineering of the thermophilic filamentous fungus *Thermothelomyces heterothallica* for direct cellobionate production from alkali-pretreated wheat straw without the addition of external cellulases.

Using a ribonucleoprotein (RNP)-based CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing system, eight β-glucosidase genes, one cellobiose/cellobionate phosphorylase gene, and two putative cellobionate transporter genes were sequentially disrupted to construct the engineered TH11 strain.

Under optimized fermentation conditions (43 °C and 50 mM citrate buffer, pH 6.0), the engineered strain produced approximately 64 mM cellobionate within five days.

The study achieved approximately 88% cellulose conversion, demonstrating the potential of fungal metabolic engineering for sustainable bioconversion of lignocellulosic biomass into value-added bioproducts.

### Key Findings

- Development of the engineered fungal strain TH11.
- CRISPR-Cas9-mediated disruption of 11 target genes.
- Direct cellobionate production from pretreated wheat straw.
- Approximately 64 mM cellobionate production in five days.
- Approximately 88% cellulose conversion without external cellulases.

